B('acquiescence', 'Acquiescence bias', 'L. Cronbach', 1946, 'Tendency to agree with statements regardless of content.', 'Survey items should be balanced (mixed positive/negative wording, as in SUS).', 'Are survey items balanced to counter yes-saying?', sub='Research bias')
B('recency', 'Recency bias', 'Memory research', '1960s', 'Recent events weigh more heavily in judgment.', 'Post-task questionnaires are dominated by the last task; randomize task order.', 'Is task order randomized in testing?', sub='Research bias')
B('primacy', 'Primacy effect', 'S. Asch', 1946, 'Early information has outsized influence on impressions.', 'First screens and first impressions shape judgments of the whole product.', 'Is the first screen strong and clear?')
B('framing-question', 'Question-order and wording effects', 'H. Schuman & S. Presser', 1981, 'The order and wording of survey questions change responses.', 'Write neutral questions and randomize where appropriate.', 'Are survey questions neutral and well-ordered?', sub='Research bias')
B('ambiguity-aversion', 'Ambiguity aversion', 'D. Ellsberg', 1961, 'People prefer known risks over unknown risks.', 'Unclear pricing, delivery dates or outcomes reduce conversion; be specific.', 'Are costs, timelines and outcomes stated specifically?')
B('zero-risk', 'Zero-risk bias', 'J. Baron, R. Gowda & H. Kunreuther', 1993, 'Preference for eliminating a small risk entirely over reducing a larger risk.', 'Guarantees and free returns remove perceived risk and increase conversion.', 'Are guarantees and return policies visible at decision points?')
B('ostrich', 'Ostrich effect', 'D. Galai & O. Sade', 2006, 'Avoiding negative information.', 'Users avoid checking finances/health dashboards when news may be bad; design supportive framing.', 'Is negative information presented supportively with next steps?', conf='medium')
B('optimism-time', 'Present bias in subscriptions (trial traps)', 'S. DellaVigna & U. Malmendier', 2006, 'People overestimate future usage and forget to cancel ("paying not to go to the gym").', 'Auto-renewing trials exploit this; send reminders and make cancellation easy.', 'Are trial end reminders sent and cancellation easy?')
B('choice-blindness', 'Choice blindness', 'P. Johansson, L. Hall et al.', 2005, 'People often fail to notice when the outcome differs from their choice and justify it anyway.', 'Always show a clear summary of chosen options before commitment.', 'Is there a clear summary of selections before checkout/submit?', conf='medium')
B('framing-default-effects-privacy', 'Privacy paradox', 'S. Barnes; P. Norberg et al.', '2006/2007', 'People state strong privacy concerns but disclose personal data readily.', 'Do not interpret disclosure as consent to misuse; design privacy-protective defaults.', 'Are privacy-protective settings default?')
B('control-illusion', 'Illusion of control', 'E. Langer', 1975, 'People overestimate their control over outcomes.', 'Placebo controls (buttons that do nothing) are deceptive; real controls must work.', 'Do all controls (e.g., close buttons, settings) actually do what they claim?')
B('automation-bias', 'Automation bias', 'K. Mosier & L. Skitka', 1996, 'Over-reliance on automated suggestions.', 'Show AI confidence and sources.', 'Do AI outputs show uncertainty and evidence?')
B('anthropomorphism', 'Anthropomorphism / ELIZA effect', 'J. Weizenbaum', 1966, 'People attribute human understanding and emotion to computer programs that mimic conversation.', 'Users over-trust chatbots; disclose that it is an AI and its limits.', 'Does the conversational agent disclose that it is an AI and its limitations?')
B('negativity', 'Negativity bias', 'P. Rozin & E. Royzman', 2001, 'Negative experiences have a greater impact than positive ones.', 'One bad interaction outweighs several good ones; prioritize fixing pain points.', 'Are severe pain points prioritized over adding delight?')
B('bizarreness', 'Bizarreness effect', 'M. McDaniel & G. Einstein', 1986, 'Unusual material is remembered better.', 'Novel visuals aid memorability but harm learnability of conventional controls.', 'Is novelty used for content/brand rather than for core controls?', conf='medium')
B('humor', 'Humor effect', 'Memory research', '1990s', 'Humorous items are remembered better.', 'Playful microcopy can aid memory but is inappropriate in error/serious contexts.', 'Is tone appropriate to context (no jokes in errors involving money, health, loss)?', conf='medium')
B('cheerleader', 'Cheerleader effect', 'D. Walker & E. Vul', 2014, 'Faces appear more attractive in a group than alone.', 'Group product imagery can raise appeal; minor relevance.', '', conf='medium')
B('framing-tunnel', 'Tunnel vision under scarcity', 'S. Mullainathan & E. Shafir', 2013, 'Scarcity of time or money captures attention and reduces cognitive bandwidth.', 'Users under stress (debt, illness, urgency) have less capacity; simplify services for them.', 'Are services used under stress (benefits, health, debt) especially simple?')
B('decision-fatigue', 'Decision fatigue / ego depletion', 'R. Baumeister et al.; S. Danziger et al.', '1998/2011', 'Making many decisions may degrade the quality of later decisions (replication of ego depletion is contested).', 'Reduce the number of decisions in long flows; offer sensible defaults.', 'Are long flows reduced to the decisions that truly matter, with good defaults?', conf='medium')
B('affect-heuristic', 'Affect heuristic', 'P. Slovic et al.', 2002, 'Emotions shape judgments of risks and benefits.', 'Visual tone and imagery influence trust and perceived risk.', 'Does the emotional tone match the gravity of the task?')
B('framing-feature-positive', 'Feature-positive effect', 'H. Jenkins & R. Sainsbury', 1970, 'People notice the presence of features more easily than their absence.', 'Users miss what is NOT included in a plan; show exclusions clearly in comparisons.', 'Do comparison tables clearly show what is not included?', conf='medium')
B('framing-unit', 'Unit bias', 'A. Geier, P. Rozin & G. Doros', 2006, 'People consider one unit of something an appropriate amount.', 'Default quantities and portion sizes shape choices.', 'Are default quantities reasonable for the user?', conf='medium')
B('framing-self-serving', 'Self-serving bias', 'D. Miller & M. Ross', 1975, 'People attribute successes to themselves and failures to external factors.', 'Users blame the product for errors; error messages should never blame the user.', 'Do error messages avoid blaming users?')
B('learned-helplessness', 'Learned helplessness', 'M. Seligman', 1967, 'After repeated uncontrollable failures, people stop trying.', 'Repeated unexplained errors teach users they cannot succeed; provide clear recovery and support routes.', 'Do repeated failures lead users to a working alternative (support, human help)?')
B('framing-choice-architecture-sludge', 'Sludge', 'R. Thaler; C. Sunstein', '2018/2020', 'Excessive friction that makes beneficial actions harder (claiming refunds, cancelling, opting out).', 'Audit flows for asymmetric friction between beneficial and costly actions.', 'Is it as easy to cancel/opt-out/claim as it was to sign up?')
B('reflection', 'Cognitive reflection / intuitive errors', 'S. Frederick', 2005, 'Many people go with an intuitive wrong answer unless prompted to reflect.', 'For consequential choices, prompt reflection (summaries, comparisons).', 'Are consequential choices supported with summaries and comparisons?', conf='medium')
B('framing-temporal', 'Fresh start effect', 'H. Dai, K. Milkman & J. Riis', 2014, 'People are more likely to pursue goals after temporal landmarks (new year, birthdays, Mondays).', 'Time goal prompts and re-engagement to temporal landmarks.', 'Are motivational prompts timed to meaningful moments?')
B('framing-identifiable-victim', 'Identifiable victim effect', 'T. Schelling; D. Small & G. Loewenstein', '1968/2003', 'People respond more to a single identified person than to statistics.', 'Stories motivate (donations, empathy) but must be honest.', 'Are stories genuine and representative?', conf='medium')
B('framing-pratfall', 'Pratfall effect', 'E. Aronson, B. Willerman & J. Floyd', 1966, 'Competent people become more likable after making a small mistake.', 'Honest admissions of limits (e.g., an AI saying it may be wrong) can increase trust.', 'Does the product honestly communicate its limitations?', conf='medium')
B('framing-weber-pricing', 'Left-digit effect (charm pricing)', 'M. Thomas & V. Morwitz', 2005, 'Prices ending in .99 are perceived as much lower when the leftmost digit changes.', 'Common pricing tactic; not deceptive per se, but contributes to price opacity.', '', conf='high')
B('framing-quantification', 'Precision effect in numbers', 'M. Janiszewski & D. Uy', 2008, 'Precise numbers are perceived as more credible and act as stronger anchors.', 'Specific claims ("saves 37 minutes") are trusted more than round numbers; must be true.', 'Are precise claims backed by data?', conf='medium')
B('framing-noble-edge', 'Noble edge effect', 'A. Chernev & S. Blair', 2015, 'Corporate social responsibility can make products seem better performing.', 'Sustainability claims affect perceived quality; greenwashing is deceptive.', 'Are sustainability claims substantiated?', conf='medium')
B('framing-belief', 'Belief bias', 'J. Evans, J. Barston & P. Pollard', 1983, 'Judging arguments by the believability of conclusions rather than logic.', 'Teams may accept weak research that confirms beliefs.', '', sub='Research bias', conf='medium')
B('framing-observer-hawthorne', 'Hawthorne effect', 'H. Landsberger', 1958, 'Behavior changes when observed.', 'Validate lab findings with real-world data.', 'Are lab findings validated with analytics?', sub='Research bias')
